<b>Interview 7: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;： male, poet, born in 1950s, Beijing (monolog)</b>

<p>I belong to the post-50s, 10 years later than the Republic. My father fought for the Republic with General Lin Biao, from northeast to Beijing, therefore I live in Beijing. I like the post-80s and I am prepared to like the post-90s. I don’t repulse the post-70s and I just go-by the post-60s. For me success is about being “Niubi” (Chinese slang for “excellent”), that is, to have friends and money and everybody in the street knows you. I am a Marxist atheist, I don’t believe in Christ or Buddha.
<p>I started to write poem in 1983. That’s when the “misty poetry” was most popular. Back then we call it “gangster poetry”. From “Yuan Ming Yuan” to “Survivor Poets Club”, from Beidao to Shizhi, these were the Beijing poets of the time. We are the third generation outside of Beijing because we are the post-60s. Beidao is old, he was born in 1949. Lots of people including Mangke are post-50s. Then and there we wanted to make the best poetry. For us, it’s ok to be a jerk but your poems must be “Niubi”.
<p>Then the society began to be commercialized and many poets began to write novels. Poems don’t make money and we need to make a living. Lots of poets were jobless. <i>Poem Magazine<i> and <i>People Literature<i> pay 15 yuan per thousand words for poetry as well as novel. A piece of poetry, 14 lines, amounts to 15 yuan. In 1980s 10 yuan could make a long time. The best time for poetry was 1983-1989, but after June 4th 1989 I stopped writing poems. Because of the market economy writing poems is not as profit as writing about football. So I wrote about football. I just like to change myself… Now I like writing blogs and essays. I didn’t write any poem from 1992 to 2007. Now I am middle aged and the feeling is back. I start to compose poetry again.
<p>Nie Lu Da said same famous words. This fellow died in 1970s and didn’t see the 21st century. He said “Are we going to hold a funeral for poetry in the 21st century?” I think not. But we always know poets are endangered race. There can never be a good number of poets. Other kinds of artist could be numerous but not poets.
<p>I have read <i>Interviews of the 80s<i> by Cha Jian Ying. What she wrote is the old 80s excerpt Cui Jian, Cui Jian is even younger than me. He was born in 1962. We poets of the 1980s were all born in 1960s and were only about 20 years old in 1980s. We don’t have the sense of mission of the old 80s but we have the sense of hippie and the strongest aspiration for art. We wrote things that are “Niubi” but don’t have any political or religious images.
<p>Poetry is about youthhood. When you grow up you just lose it, like Rimbaud and Dylan Thomas. Another kind of Poetry belongs to the wise, such as Borges and Rilke, and that’s the older the better. Writing poems can train your language sense. Commercial or column essays are simply for money. Those words are of no strength.
<p>I don’t fool around anymore, at least not in night clubs of Beijing. Sometimes I have a dinner party with my friends and have some alcohol. I am a friend of &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;, we went to Halar together. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; has told us there’s no restriction in Kassel and we are not supposed to do anything but to fool around just like we normally do in Beijing. In Kassel, we read Tagore’s poem and Hegel’s books. We sing Tengger’s songs and wear Youngor’s clothes.
<p>Actually I know all these people. We often eat and drink and get drunk and dawdle around. It’s quite normal in Beijing. But this time we have flown over 8000 miles and moved our party from Beijing to Kassel. We “dawdle around to Germany”, just a change of place.
<p>Germany. I respect this country very much. It’s the originator of the two world wars. I always think Germany is the heart of Europe. Europe is big and Germany is right in the center. And Kassel is the heart of Germany. It feels good that we came to the heart of Germany and the Germans just leave us alone.

